<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cyprus</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Nicosia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An island republic in the eastern Mediterranean, independent since 1960 and divided since 1974, with the north held by the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cyprus became independent on 16 August 1960, after an armed campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoka">EOKA</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-cyprus">British Cyprus</a> and under the Zürich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Agreements. Greek Cypriots had sought enosis, union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, while Turkish Cypriots wanted partition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept the Sovereign Base Areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> and Dhekelia, and Greece, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and Britain received guarantor rights.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makarios-iii">Archbishop Makarios</a> III was the first president. The constitution shared power between the communities, but disputes led to violence from 21 December 1963, in which 364 Turkish and 174 Greek Cypriots died, and Turkish Cypriots withdrew from government. On 15 July 1974 a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> staged a coup against Makarios, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> invaded on 20 July and again on 14 August. Some 180,000 Greek Cypriots and about 50,000 Turkish Cypriots were displaced, and Turkish forces took about 36% of the island. The Turkish Cypriot north later became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-cyprus">Northern Cyprus</a>, recognised only by Turkey.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Cyprus joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on 1 May 2004, with EU law suspended in the north. That year 74% of Greek Cypriots rejected the Annan Plan, which 65% of Turkish Cypriots supported. Reunification talks relaunched in 2015 collapsed in 2017. Dimitris Christofias became the first Communist head of state in 2008, Nicos Anastasiades president in 2013, and Nikos Christodoulides was sworn in on 28 February 2023.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cyprus.jpg" alt="Cyprus" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: İsmet Güney, SVG by Vzb83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cyprus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicosia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Makarios III, Glafkos Clerides, Dimitris Christofias, Nicos Anastasiades, Nikos Christodoulides</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek Orthodox, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Cyprus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=cyprus" aria-label="Cyprus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Cyprus lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5235 4555L5235 4555M5235 4555L5235 4553"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5235 4555h0M5235 4553h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 4555h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 4555h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nicosia. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=cyprus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
